# Cursor Rules — Attack-Agnostic Generalization Robustness Project

## Project Context
This is an academic thesis project studying whether adversarial training against one
attack type (PGD) generalizes to unseen attack types (FGSM, MIM, C&W, black-box
surrogate-transfer), stratified across SNR levels, on RadioML 2016.10a. Reference
`PRD.md`, `architecture.md`, `design.md`, and `phases.md` in the repo root before making
structural suggestions. The core deliverable is the generalization-gap heatmap
(attack type x SNR) — treat any change touching `evaluate.py` or `attacks.py` as
high-stakes for this reason.

## Stack (do not suggest alternatives without strong justification)
- Python 3.10+, PyTorch (not TensorFlow)
- numpy, scipy for signal preprocessing
- torchattacks for FGSM, PGD, MIM, C&W (do not hand-implement attack math — use the
  library's implementations for consistency with cited literature methodology)
- pandas, matplotlib, seaborn for analysis/plots
- No web framework, no database, no Docker, no foundation-model dependencies (explicitly
  out of scope per PRD.md Section 2.2) — do not suggest adding infrastructure not already
  in `requirements.txt`.

## File/Module Boundaries (respect ownership — see phases.md team split)
- `data_loader.py` / `preprocess.py` — Person A's files. Data parsing and splitting only.
  SNR metadata must survive every transformation — never write code that drops it.
- `model_baseline.py` — standard CNN architecture only, no training loop logic here.
- `model_robust.py` — same base architecture as baseline; adversarial training logic
  belongs in `train.py`, not duplicated here.
- `model_surrogate.py` — must be architecturally DIFFERENT from `model_baseline.py`
  (different depth/kernel sizes or a different model family). Never import or share
  weights from `model_baseline.py` — it must only be trained on query-response pairs, to
  keep the black-box threat model realistic.
- `train.py` — the ONE shared generic training loop. Supports both plain supervised
  training and PGD-adversarial training via a hook (e.g. `adversarial_fn=None`), not via
  duplicated copies of the loop.
- `attacks.py` — FGSM, PGD, MIM, C&W (all via `torchattacks`), plus the black-box
  surrogate-transfer logic. Keep white-box attacks (gradients through the real target) and
  the black-box path (gradients through the surrogate, examples transferred to the real
  target) clearly separated in code — this distinction is easy to accidentally blur and
  would invalidate the black-box claim if done wrong.
- `evaluate.py` — accuracy-vs-SNR, confusion matrix, and the generalization-gap heatmap.
  Always evaluate per-SNR-bucket, never only an aggregate accuracy number. Keep the
  generalization-gap computation (robust model's PGD performance vs. its performance
  against every unseen attack) in one clearly isolated, well-named function.
- `utils.py` — seed control, plotting helpers, checkpoint I/O. Keep it dependency-light.
- `frontend/app.py` — Streamlit demo (Demo tab) + results dashboard (Dashboard tab). The
  Demo tab may call into `attacks.py` and load model checkpoints directly for live
  prediction. The Dashboard tab must only read `results/metrics.json` and
  `results/plots/` — it must never re-run training or attacks itself. Default to
  **static, pre-rendered charts** in the Dashboard tab; interactive Plotly is an optional
  Week-9-only upgrade, never a blocking dependency for any other phase (see phases.md
  Phase 7 and design.md Section 11).
- `config.yaml` — all hyperparameters live here, including per-attack epsilon values and
  the C&W evaluation subset size. Never hardcode these inline in scripts.

## Coding Conventions
- Type hints on all function signatures.
- Every function that trains or evaluates must accept a `config` dict/object rather than
  individual hardcoded hyperparameters.
- Always set and log a random seed (`utils.set_seed`) at the start of any training or
  attack script.
- Checkpoint models every few epochs, not just at the end — Colab free-tier GPU
  disconnects mid-run are expected, not exceptional.
- When writing attack code: operate on the model in `eval()` mode, set
  `requires_grad=True` on the *input tensor*, never on model weights.
- C&W attack is expensive — never run it on the full test set by default; respect
  `cw_eval_subset_size` from `config.yaml`.
- Preserve per-sample SNR metadata through every preprocessing and attack step.

## What NOT to do
- Do not suggest swapping PyTorch for TensorFlow or JAX.
- Do not suggest a frontend beyond `frontend/app.py`'s two defined tabs (Demo, Dashboard)
  — no additional pages, no user auth, no backend API server. The frontend is a thin
  presentation layer over existing artifacts, not a product.
- Do not let the Dashboard tab re-run training or attacks — it reads `metrics.json` and
  `results/plots/` only. Live computation belongs in the Demo tab exclusively.
- Do not default the Dashboard tab to interactive Plotly — static charts are the committed
  deliverable; interactive is an opt-in Week 9 stretch only.
- Do not suggest foundation-model-based approaches (RFPrompt, pretrained wireless
  backbones), autoencoder-based signal purification, or dual-domain contrastive learning —
  explicitly deferred per PRD.md Section 2.2, each is independently thesis-scale.
- Do not suggest channel-aware/over-the-air attack modeling as core scope — it's an
  optional stretch goal only (design.md Section 10), never a blocking dependency for the
  main deliverable.
- Do not commit large files: `data/raw/`, `data/processed/`, `checkpoints/*.pt` are
  gitignored — never suggest tracking these in git.
- Do not silently change the train/val/test split logic — it must stay stratified by
  `(modulation, SNR)`.
- Do not let `model_surrogate.py` share code/weights with `model_baseline.py` — this
  would invalidate the black-box threat model.

## Git Workflow Awareness
- Three branches: `person-a-data`, `person-b-attacks`, `person-c-eval`, merging into
  `main` at phase boundaries (see phases.md). When suggesting commits, keep them scoped
  to the current branch owner's files.
- Use `--no-ff` merges into `main`.

## When Suggesting Code
- Prefer small, testable functions over large scripts.
- Flag (in comments or chat) any place where C&W evaluation or PGD-based adversarial
  training will significantly increase runtime — both are known project risks.
- If asked to add a new dependency or research direction, check it against the "explicitly
  not pursued" list in PRD.md Section 2.2 before proceeding.
